cont_update=Update
cont_help=What do these actions do? Start, Stop and Restart control whether a container runs. NOTE: Restart does NOT load a new version from your compose/.env files - use the bold Update link (shown for Compose projects) for that. Pause freezes a container without stopping it, Kill force-stops it immediately, and Remove deletes the container itself (you will see a red confirmation first; data stored in volumes is kept).

compose_intro=Each project below is a Docker Compose application (usually one website or service made of several containers). Use the buttons on each row - you never need to type file paths for existing projects.
compose_domain=Domain
compose_update=Update
compose_restart=Restart
compose_stop=Stop
compose_start=Start
compose_logs2=Logs
compose_ps2=Status
compose_down2=Down (remove containers)
compose_down_btn=Down
compose_pull=Pull images only
compose_legend=What do these buttons do?
compose_update_desc=Downloads the new version set in the project's compose/.env files and recreates its containers. Data stored in volumes (databases, uploads, settings) is kept. Use this after editing .env or docker-compose.yml.
compose_restart_desc=Restarts the project's containers. This does NOT apply a new version - use Update for that.
compose_stop_desc=Stops the project's containers. The website/app goes OFFLINE until started again. Nothing is deleted.
compose_start_desc=Starts the project's stopped containers again.
compose_readonly_desc=Safe, read-only: show recent log lines / current container status.
compose_down_desc=Stops AND removes the project's containers. The app stays offline until you run Update or Up again. Data in volumes is kept, but only use this if you know why you need it.
compose_update_note=Changed a version number in .env or docker-compose.yml and the app is still on the old version? That is normal - a restart never applies new versions. Click Update.
compose_noproject=No Compose projects were found on this system.
compose_advanced=Advanced: run an action on a compose file by path
compose_abs_hint=Enter the FULL path to the file, for example /home/user/domains/example.com/public_html/docker-compose.yml - a bare "docker-compose.yml" will not be found.
compose_down_volumes_warn=DANGER: also deletes the project's volumes - databases and uploaded files are permanently lost!
compose_project_name=Project name (optional)
compose_project_hint=Leave empty to let Docker derive the name from the file/directory. Fill it in (lowercase) to target an EXACT existing stack - this guarantees Docker updates that stack in place, reusing its containers and volumes, instead of creating a second copy.
compose_result=Compose: $1

confirm_details=Exactly this will be affected:
confirm_compose_update=Update project $1?
confirm_compose_update_desc=Docker will download the image versions currently set in the compose/.env files of project $1 and recreate its containers. Data in volumes is preserved. The app will be briefly unavailable while containers restart.
confirm_compose_stop_title=Stop project $1?
confirm_compose_stop=The application $1 will go OFFLINE until it is started again. Nothing will be deleted.
confirm_compose_down_title=Remove the containers of project $1?
confirm_compose_down=Project $1 will go OFFLINE and its containers will be removed. Data in volumes is kept. Use Update or Up to bring it back online.
confirm_compose_down_vol=DANGER: this removes the containers AND their volumes - databases and uploaded files will be PERMANENTLY LOST. Only continue if you have a backup.

update_title2=Update $1
update_err_noproject=Unknown Compose project
update_what=What will happen
update_point1=Docker downloads the image versions written in this project's docker-compose.yml and .env files
update_point2=The project's containers are recreated with the new version
update_point3=Named volumes (databases, uploads, settings) are NOT deleted - your data is kept
update_point4=The application will be briefly offline while the containers restart
update_note_restart=Why not just Restart? A restart keeps the old version - Docker only reads .env and docker-compose.yml when containers are (re)created, which is exactly what Update does.
update_pinned=The module tells Docker the exact project name ($1), so this EXISTING stack is upgraded in place - same containers, same volumes, same data. No second copy can be created.
update_files=Compose files
update_containers=Containers in this project
update_button=Update now

maint_intro=This page shows how much disk space Docker uses and lets you clean up. Cleanup permanently deletes data - each section explains in red exactly what it removes, the preview below shows what would be deleted right now, and a final red confirmation lists everything again before anything happens.
maint_df_note=Reclaimable is the space you could free with the cleanup actions below.
maint_preview_heading=What would be deleted right now
maint_preview_note=This list is refreshed every time you open this page. Items marked in red are databases or belong to a live domain - double-check those before cleaning up.
maint_preview_containers=Stopped containers (deleted by System prune)
maint_preview_volumes=Volumes not used by any container
maint_preview_dangling=Leftover image layers (deleted by prune)
maint_preview_unused_images=Images not used by any container (deleted only with "include all unused")
maint_none_containers=No stopped containers - nothing to delete here.
maint_none_volumes=No unused volumes.
maint_none_dangling=No leftover image layers.
maint_none_unused=Every image is in use.
maint_nothing=Nothing would be deleted right now.
maint_tag_db=DATABASE - contains data!
maint_tag_domain=belongs to domain $1
maint_tag_anon=will be deleted by prune
maint_tag_named=named volume - prune keeps it (remove manually under Storage if really unwanted)
maint_sysprune_desc=Deletes ALL stopped containers, unused networks, leftover image layers and the build cache. Running containers and data in named volumes are not touched - but a stopped container you meant to start again WILL be gone.
maint_sysprune_all_desc=Also deletes EVERY image not used by a container - they must be downloaded again next time, which can be slow.
maint_sysprune_vol_desc=DANGER: also deletes unused anonymous volumes. If a stopped container kept data (for example a database) in one of them, that data is PERMANENTLY LOST.
maint_builder_desc=Deletes Docker's build cache only. Safe: no containers, images in use, or data are affected - future image builds are just slower the first time.
maint_confirm_note=Clicking a button above never deletes immediately - a red confirmation page listing exactly what will be removed always comes first.

dup_heading=Possible old copy of an application detected
dup_line=$1 (image $2, serving $3) is a standalone container, while Compose project $4 runs the same application. Visitors of the domain still see the OLD copy until it is removed and the website proxy port matches the new project's port.
dup_nodomain=no proxied domain
dup_data_note=IMPORTANT: each copy keeps its data in its OWN volumes - a newly created project starts with an EMPTY database. Before removing the old copy, make sure the new one really has your data (log in and check), or move it first with Backup / Restore under Storage.

stor_remove_warn=Removing a volume PERMANENTLY deletes everything stored inside it (databases, uploads, settings). Make a backup below first.
stor_prune_vol_note=Prune only removes unused ANONYMOUS volumes (the ones with long random names). Named project volumes are never touched by prune.

